'''Abu Abd al-Rahman Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi''' ({{langx|ar|أبو عبد الرحمن موسى بن علي بن رباح اللخمي}}) ({{circa|707}}-779/80)<ref name="1a">{{harvnb|Gordon|Robinson|Rowson|Fishbein|2018|p=1149}}; {{harnvb|Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani|1968|p=363}}; {{harvnb|Ibn Taghribirdi|1930|p=26}}</ref> was an [[Faqīh|Islamic scholar]].

==Career==
Musa was born in [[Ifriqiya|North Africa]]<ref name="1a" /> to [[Ulayy ibn Rabah al-Lakhmi]], an early [[hadith]] narrator and [[Umayyad Caliphate|Umayyad]] confidant. His father's name had originally been Ali, but was changed to Ulayy in order to escape anti-[[Alids|Alid]] sentiment in the Umayyad era.<ref>On Ulayy see {{harvnb|Raisuddin|1993|pp=29 ff.}}; {{harvnb|Clarke|2012|pp=36 ff.}}; {{harvnb|Ibn Sa'd|1997|p=317}}.</ref>

During his lifetime Musa narrated hadith on the authority of his father, as well as from [[Ibn Shihab al-Zuhri]], [[Muhammad ibn Munkadir]], [[Yazid ibn Abi Habib]], [[Yazid ibn Abi Mansur]], and [[Hibban ibn Abi Jabalah]].<ref>{{harvnb|Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani|1968|p=363}}; {{harvnb|Ibn Taghribirdi|1930|p=26}}.</ref> He was considered a highly reliable (''[[Hadith studies#Biographical evaluation|thiqa thiqa]]'') hadith transmitter by the traditionalist [[Ahmad ibn Hanbal]] and "reliable, God willing" (''thiqa-[[Inshallah|in-sha'a llah]]'') by [[Ibn Sa'd]].{{sfn|Lucas|2004|pp=307, 318}}

In 772 Musa was selected by the terminally ill governor of [[Egypt]], [[Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj al-Tujibi|Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Tujibi]], to succeed him upon his death, and he was subsequently confirmed in that position by the [[Abbasid Caliphate|Abbasid]] caliph [[al-Mansur]]. He remained as governor over the next six years, during which he put down a [[Copts|Coptic]] revolt near [[Rosetta|Rashid]] in 773, before being dismissed by the caliph [[al-Mahdi]] in 778.<ref>{{harvnb|Al-Kindi|1912|pp=118, 119–20}}; {{harvnb|Ibn Taghribirdi|1930|pp=23, 25 ff.}}; {{harvnb|Morimoto|1981|p=150}}. See also {{harvnb|Kennedy|1981|pp=33–34 n. 46}}, for a reconciliation of Musa's governorship with conflicting information provided by [[al-Tabari]].</ref>

He died in 779/780 in [[Alexandria]].<ref>{{harvnb|Gordon|Robinson|Rowson|Fishbein|2018|p=1149}}; {{harvnb|Ibn Hajar al-Asqalani|1968|p=363}}; {{harvnb|Ibn Taghribirdi|1930|p=26}}; {{harvnb|Khalifah ibn Khayyat|1985|p=437}}.</ref>

